      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Coronary restenosis, a major complication of percutaneous balloon angioplasty, results from neointimal proliferation of vascular smooth muscle cells (VSMCs). The sarco/endoplasmic reticulum calcium ATPase 2a isoform (SERCA2a), specific to contractile VSMCs, has been reported previously to be involved in the control of the Ca(2+)-signaling pathways governing proliferation and migration. Moreover, SERCA2a gene transfer was reported to inhibit in vitro VSMC proliferation and to prevent neointimal thickening in a rat carotid injury model. The aim of this study was to evaluate the potential therapeutic interest of SERCA2a gene transfer for prevention of in-stent restenosis using a ex vivo model of human left internal mammary artery (hIMA) intimal thickening. Left hIMAs, obtained at the time of aorto-coronary bypass surgeries, were subjected to balloon dilatation followed by infection for 30 min with adenoviruses encoding either human SERCA2 and green fluorescence protein (GFP) or control gene (β-galactosidase, β-gal) and GFP. Proliferation of subendothelial VSMCs and neointimal thickening were observed in balloon-injured hIMA maintained 14 days in organ culture under constant pressure and perfusion. SERCA2a gene transfer prevented vascular remodeling and significantly (P<0.01, n=5) reduced neointimal thickening in injured arteries (intima/media ratio was 0.07±0.01 vs 0.40±0.03 in β-gal-infected arteries). These findings could have potential implications for treatment of pathological in-stent restenosis."},
